# Conductor: nightly red - critical Next.js RCE, then the expired catalog fixture - 2026-09-10
#
# DISPATCH: ./scripts/dispatch.sh git@github.com:Arlencho/olympus-platform.git wave-plans/conductor/2026-09-10-nightly-red-next-rce-and-catalog.plan --auto --retries 2
#
# SERIALIZED. Waves 1-2 are the security bump and are urgent. Waves 3-4 are the
# time-dependent test fixture. Separate branches, separate PRs.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1 | devops | SECURITY. Clear the critical Next.js advisories failing the nightly npm audit. THE FINDING, reproduced locally today, so do not re-derive it: cd apps/web then npm audit --audit-level=moderate exits 1 with 3 vulnerabilities, 1 high and 2 CRITICAL, all in next, affected range 16.0.0 to 16.3.2. The two criticals are Unauthenticated Remote Code Execution on windows-hosted servers, advisory GHSA-p293-qw3h-jr36, and Unauthenticated Remote Code Execution in the Image Optimization API when AVIF files are used, advisory GHSA-2xp9-vwfh-vxw4. apps/web/package.json line 30 declares next at caret 16.2.11. npm reports a fix is available. ASSESS EXPOSURE FIRST and put it in the PR body, because it decides urgency and you should not overstate or understate it: our web tier deploys to Vercel on Linux, so the windows-hosted RCE is very likely not reachable for us, but the Image Optimization AVIF path is platform independent, so state plainly whether next/image is used in this app and whether AVIF is among the configured or accepted formats. Read apps/web/next.config.ts and grep for next/image usage rather than assuming either way. THE FIX: bump next to the lowest version that clears BOTH criticals and the high, staying within a range the app actually supports. Prefer an explicit version bump in package.json plus a lockfile update over running npm audit fix blind, so the diff states exactly which version we moved to and why. If the only clearing version is outside the current major or requires a codemod, STOP and report that in a comment rather than forcing it. Update the lockfile at the workspace ROOT, which is where the only package-lock.json lives, and never bump apps/web/package.json without it: pointing dependency tooling at apps/web alone silently desyncs the two. VERIFY, pasting real exit codes captured directly rather than read after a pipe, because a pipe returns the exit code of the last command and not the one you are testing: cd apps/web then npm audit --audit-level=moderate must exit 0, then npx tsc --noEmit, then the vitest suite, then a production build. A build that fails after a framework bump is the whole risk here, so do not skip it. Also run the E2E critical subset if it can be run locally, and say so if it cannot. Open a PR titled as a security bump, referencing the two advisory IDs in the body. Omit any Co-Authored-By trailer, and do not put any AI tool or vendor name in the commit message, the PR title or the PR body. Do not merge. | fix/next-rce-advisories

2 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 1 change on branch fix/next-rce-advisories. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: is the vulnerability actually gone, and did clearing it break the app. FIRST, run npm audit yourself against the branch and confirm it exits 0 at the moderate threshold, and confirm specifically that both critical advisories and the high are absent rather than merely that the count dropped. A partial clear that still leaves a critical is the failure mode. SECOND, confirm the lockfile and package.json agree, and that the lockfile at the workspace root was updated rather than only the app manifest, since a desync there ships a different tree than the one that was tested. THIRD, a framework bump is a build risk, not just a dependency line, so confirm the production build and the type check were actually run and actually passed on this exact head, reproducing at least the build yourself rather than accepting a pasted result. FOURTH, check the diff for anything beyond the dependency change: a bump PR that also edits application code, config or CSP has widened into something that needs a different review. FIFTH, confirm the version moved to is genuinely the fixed line for both advisories rather than a version that merely happens to be newer. SIXTH, confirm no AI tool or vendor name appears anywhere in the commit message, PR title or PR body, and no Co-Authored-By trailer. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC NEXT RCE BUMP, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE, per-finding severity and file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | fix/next-rce-advisories

3 | go-backend | Fix a time-dependent test fixture that just turned the nightly Go suite red on main with no code change. THE ROOT CAUSE, already diagnosed and reproduced locally, so implement it rather than re-deriving it: apps/api/internal/inventory/report_test.go line 18 sets cat.Meta.FetchedAt to a hardcoded time.Date(2026, 8, 11, 0, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC). DefaultCatalogMaxAge at apps/api/internal/inventory/catalog.go line 51 is 30 times 24 hours. IsStale at catalog.go line 186 is time.Since(FetchedAt) greater than that max age. Today is 2026-09-10, exactly 30 days after the fixture date, so the fixture crossed the staleness threshold overnight and the IsStale arm of DecisionHolds at report.go now returns false. The visible failure is TestMunicipalityNamingDoesNotMoveTheVerdict at naming_2513_test.go line 145, which asserts a naming observation must not void a section 24.4 verdict. Nothing about naming is broken: the catalog simply went stale under it. THE FIX: make the fixture date relative to now rather than pinned, so it cannot expire again. Every other test in this package already does this, so follow the existing local convention rather than inventing one: see critic_round2_test.go line 230, mutation_pins_test.go lines 181 and 224, and report_test.go line 261, all of which use time.Now().UTC(). Note that critic_falseclean_test.go line 230 deliberately sets a date a year in the past to test the STALE path, so it must not be touched: a deliberately stale fixture and an accidentally stale one are different things and only the accidental one is a bug. BEFORE YOU CHANGE ANYTHING, check every caller of testCatalog and confirm none of them depends on the catalog being stale, since five test files use it. Say in the PR body which you checked. ALSO SWEEP for the same defect class rather than fixing only the instance that fired: grep the whole apps/api tree for hardcoded absolute dates in test fixtures that are compared against time.Now, and list what you find in the PR body. Fix only the ones that are latent bugs of this exact shape, name any you deliberately left, and do not widen the diff into unrelated cleanup. PROVE IT: run the failing test on the parent commit and show it FAILING, then on your branch and show it passing, pasting both real exit codes captured directly rather than read after a pipe. Then run the whole api unit suite with the race detector, go build, go vet and golangci-lint, and paste real exit codes. Open a PR referencing the nightly tracker issue 2794 with Refs and no closing keyword, since that tracker also covers the npm audit failure handled separately. Omit any Co-Authored-By trailer, and no AI tool or vendor name anywhere in the commit or PR text. Do not merge. | fix/expired-catalog-fixture

4 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 3 change on branch fix/expired-catalog-fixture. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: can this fixture expire again, and did the fix weaken any test. FIRST, confirm the new fixture date is derived from the current time and cannot cross the staleness threshold no matter when the suite runs. A fix that merely moves the pin to a later date is NOT a fix, it is the same bug with a longer fuse, and should be reported as BLOCK-FIX. SECOND, and this is the one to grade hardest, confirm critic_falseclean_test.go was NOT touched. That test deliberately sets a catalog a year stale in order to exercise the stale path, so making it fresh would silently delete the coverage that proves staleness is detected at all. Read it and say what it asserts. THIRD, verify the claimed fail-first: check out the parent commit in a throwaway worktree, run the named test, and confirm it genuinely fails there for the staleness reason rather than for some other reason, then confirm it passes on the branch. Reproduce this yourself rather than accepting the pasted output. FOURTH, confirm no test was skipped, deleted, or had an assertion weakened to make the suite green, and that DecisionHolds and IsStale themselves are unchanged: this is a fixture bug and any edit to production code needs justifying. FIFTH, review the sweep the producer reported. Independently grep apps/api for hardcoded absolute dates in tests compared against time.Now, and say whether the producer missed any, whether anything they left unfixed is genuinely deliberate, and whether anything they DID fix was actually intentional like the false-clean case. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC EXPIRED CATALOG FIXTURE, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE, per-finding severity and file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | fix/expired-catalog-fixture
